commit ef7442baefd919e155af9716cc610e7b8ddef000
Author: Martin Sedlacek <martin.martin.sedlacek@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 13 01:28:02 2026 +0200

    Fix scene-file objects loading 100 m too high in scenes idx != 0
    
    _load_scene_prim_with_objects parks the scene prim at INITIAL_SCENE_PRIM_Z_OFFSET
    (-100) while it loads, then sets every object's pose with the default
    frame="world". The setter bakes the world target into local coordinates against
    the parked transform, so local_z = intended_z + 100; moving the prim to its final
    position afterwards carries every object up with it. The prebuilt scene structure
    keeps its own authored local transform and lands correctly, so the result is a
    room whose furniture is 100 m in the air rather than a uniformly displaced scene.
    
    Measured against REALM at num_envs=4: 70 of 128 registered objects in each of
    scenes 1..3 sat above z=50, including the breakfast table the task needs. REALM
    pins that table with a FixedJoint, which welded it up there permanently -- the
    task objects then had nothing to rest on and fell to the floor (z 0.015 instead
    of 0.82). It had been misdiagnosed as REALM's scene fixes not applying outside
    scene 0; they apply identically in every scene.
    
    Re-apply the poses once the prim is final, offset by scene_position so local
    works out to exactly the authored pose. frame="scene" would be tidier but is
    not usable here: it reads _pose_info, which Scene.load() only assigns after this
    method returns.
    
    idx == 0 is untouched (prim never parked, scene_position zero), so the
    single-scene path every production REALM eval uses is bit-identical.
    
    Verified num_envs=4: all four scenes now report above_50m=0, tables at z=0.6193
    with the x tiling preserved, and task objects on the table at z=0.82 in every
    member instead of 0.015 in scenes 1-3.

diff --git a/omnigibson/scenes/scene_base.py b/omnigibson/scenes/scene_base.py
index 15e3eb8..2fee52c 100644
--- a/omnigibson/scenes/scene_base.py
+++ b/omnigibson/scenes/scene_base.py
@@ -401,6 +401,31 @@ class Scene(Serializable, Registerable, Recreatable, ABC):
             identity_quat = th.tensor([0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0])
             self._scene_prim.set_position_orientation(position=scene_position, orientation=identity_quat)
             new_scene_edge = last_scene_edge + scene_margin + (aabb_max[0] - aabb_min[0])
+
+            # Re-apply the object poses now that the scene prim is at its final position.
+            #
+            # The loop above sets each pose with the default frame="world" while the prim is still
+            # parked at initial_scene_prim_z_offset, so the world target gets baked into local
+            # coordinates against the parked transform: local_z = intended_z - offset. Moving the
+            # prim to z=0 then carries every object with it, leaving all of them exactly -offset
+            # (i.e. +100 m) too high. The prebuilt scene structure keeps its own authored local
+            # transform and lands correctly, so the visible result is a room with its furniture
+            # missing rather than a scene that is uniformly displaced.
+            #
+            # Measured against REALM at num_envs=4: 70 of 128 registered objects in each of
+            # scenes 1..3 sat at z ~ +100, including the breakfast table the task needs. REALM then
+            # pinned that table with a FixedJoint at the lifted pose, which no later reset can undo.
+            #
+            # frame="scene" is not usable here: it routes through
+            # convert_scene_relative_pose_to_world, which reads _pose_info, and _pose_info is only
+            # assigned by Scene.load() after this method returns. So offset by scene_position
+            # explicitly -- local then works out to exactly the authored pose.
+            for obj_name, obj in self._init_objs.items():
+                obj.set_position_orientation(
+                    position=th.as_tensor(self._init_state[obj_name]["root_link"]["pos"], dtype=th.float32)
+                    + scene_position,
+                    orientation=self._init_state[obj_name]["root_link"]["ori"],
+                )
         else:
             scene_position = th.zeros(3)
             aabb_min, aabb_max = lazy.omni.usd.get_context().compute_path_world_bounding_box(scene_absolute_path)
